EX.NO. 10: JENKINS JOB TRIGGERED BY GITHUB WEBHOOK

AIM:
To configure a Jenkins job that automatically starts a build whenever code is pushed to a GitHub repository using a GitHub webhook.

GITHUB REPOSITORY:
https://github.com/sivapriyakumar246-cyber/GitExperiment1.git


STEP 1: Start Jenkins

1. Start Jenkins on your computer.
2. Open the browser and visit:

   http://localhost:8080

3. Sign in to Jenkins.

Note: GitHub must be able to reach Jenkins for webhook triggering. The ngrok tunnel must be running and forwarding to the Jenkins port (usually 8080).


STEP 2: Create a Jenkins Freestyle Job

1. On the Jenkins dashboard, click New Item.
2. Enter the job name:

   GitHub-Webhook-CI

3. Select Freestyle project.
4. Click OK.


STEP 3: Configure Source Code Management

1. In the job configuration, find Source Code Management.
2. Select Git.
3. Enter the repository URL:

   https://github.com/sivapriyakumar246-cyber/GitExperiment1.git

4. In Branch Specifier, enter:

   */main


STEP 4: Configure Build Trigger

1. Find Build Triggers.
2. Select:

   GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling

3. Do not select Trigger build remotely for this push-webhook experiment.


STEP 5: Add a Build Step

1. Find the Build section.
2. Click Add build step.
3. If Jenkins is running a Windows agent, select Execute Windows batch command.
4. Enter:

   echo Jenkins build started
   echo Checking out GitHub repository
   dir
   echo Jenkins build completed successfully

5. Click Save.


STEP 6: Configure the GitHub Webhook

1. Open the GitHub repository:

   https://github.com/sivapriyakumar246-cyber/GitExperiment1

2. Go to Settings → Webhooks.
3. Click Add webhook.
4. Enter the Payload URL:

   https://atom-quickstep-germinate.ngrok-free.dev/github-webhook/

5. Set Content type to:

   application/json

6. Select:

   Just the push event

7. Ensure Active is checked.
8. Click Add webhook.

Note: This ngrok URL works only while the tunnel is active and forwarding to Jenkins. If the ngrok URL changes, update the GitHub webhook Payload URL.


STEP 7: Test the Jenkins Job Manually

1. Open the Jenkins job GitHub-Webhook-CI.
2. Click Build Now.
3. Open the latest build from Build History.
4. Click Console Output.
5. Confirm that the build finishes with SUCCESS.


STEP 8: Push a Test Change from CMD

Open CMD and run each command separately:

cd "%USERPROFILE%\GitExperiment1\GitExperiment1"

Check the current Git status:

git status

Create a test file:

echo Experiment 10 GitHub webhook test > webhook-test.txt

Stage the file:

git add webhook-test.txt

Commit the change:

git commit -m "Test Jenkins GitHub webhook"

Push the commit:

git push origin main

If the push is rejected because GitHub has newer commits, run:

git pull --no-rebase origin main

Then push again:

git push origin main

If Git reports a merge conflict, resolve it before pushing. Do not repeat pull/push commands unnecessarily.


STEP 9: Verify the Webhook and Automatic Build

1. Open GitHub → Repository → Settings → Webhooks.
2. Click the configured webhook.
3. Check Recent Deliveries for a successful ping or push delivery.
4. Open Jenkins → GitHub-Webhook-CI.
5. Check Build History for a new build that appeared after the push.
6. Open the new build and click Console Output.
7. Confirm that it finished with SUCCESS.

A build started by clicking Build Now tests the Jenkins job manually. A new build appearing automatically after the GitHub push verifies the webhook trigger.


RESULT:
The Jenkins Freestyle job was configured with the GitHub repository, build step, and GitHub webhook. A repository push triggered Jenkins automatically, and the build execution was verified successfully.
